  "provider_query": "Python standard library documentation for the venv module",
  "answer": "Relevant sources for the Python standard library `venv` module (virtual environments):\n\n1. **Official library reference (current stable, 3.14)** — `venv` creates lightweight environments on top of a base Python install; covers CLI usage, options, and the `EnvBuilder` API. Added in 3.3. Source: `Lib/venv/`.  \n   https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/venv.html [web:1]\n\n2. **Official library reference (3.10 docs mirror)** — Same module docs: isolation from the base install, `python -m venv`, `pyvenv.cfg`, and how `sys.prefix` vs `sys.base_prefix` work. Points to PEP 405.  \n   https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html [web:8]\n\n3. **Official tutorial: Virtual Environments and Packages** — Practical guide: `python -m venv`, common `.venv` directory name, and activation on Windows (`Scripts\\activate`) and Unix/macOS (`source …/bin/activate`).  \n   https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html [web:2]\n\n4. **Dev tutorial (same chapter, in-progress docs)** — Same tutorial content for creating and activating environments with `venv`.  \n   https://docs.python.org/dev/tutorial/venv.html [web:9]\n\n5. **Tutorial source (CPython repo)** — RST source of the virtual-environments tutorial chapter.  \n   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/HEAD/Doc/tutorial/venv.rst [web:4]\n\n6. **Implementation (CPython)** — `Lib/venv/__init__.py`: `EnvBuilder`, CLI help text, and environment creation (based on PEP 405).  \n   https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/9c10d6b8000e445093e73c295ad87b5e081902f7/Lib/venv/__init__.py [web:7]\n\n7. **Overview (pydevtools handbook)** — Stdlib since 3.3; recommended since 3.5; notes `--upgrade-deps` (3.9) and that 3.12 no longer seeds `setuptools` by default.  \n   https://pydevtools.com/handbook/reference/venv/ [web:0]\n\n8. **Usage notes (version selection)** — Create an env with a chosen interpreter via `<python> -m venv <dir>`; on minimal Debian/Ubuntu installs, the `python3.x-venv` package may be required.  \n   https://www.pythontutorials.net/blog/how-do-i-use-different-python-version-in-venv-from-standard-library-not-virtualenv/ [web:5]\n\n**Quick start (from the official docs):** `python -m venv .venv`, then activate with `.venv\\Scripts\\activate` (Windows) or `source .venv/bin/activate` (Unix/macOS).",
